Comment	3:1	// Two things this fixture pins, both of which the two rings disagreed about silently
EndOfLine	3:86	\r\n
Comment	4:1	// (the corpus compiled and ran the same program; only the emitted *names* differed).
EndOfLine	4:86	\r\n
Comment	5:1	//
EndOfLine	5:3	\r\n
Comment	6:1	//   - A lambda body is *typed* like any other body, so a `for` inside one has a typed
EndOfLine	6:87	\r\n
Comment	7:1	//     loop variable, and the `smToYield` wrap a `for` puts around what it iterates is
EndOfLine	7:87	\r\n
Comment	8:1	//     the identity when that is already a machine (impl_specs/for.md).
EndOfLine	8:72	\r\n
Comment	9:1	//   - A lambda is a body of its own for the *rename* pass too: two lambdas in one body
EndOfLine	9:88	\r\n
Comment	10:1	//     may each declare a local of the same name, and both keep it. The enclosing body's
EndOfLine	10:89	\r\n
Comment	11:1	//     `used` set is not the lambda's - the same rule `rewriteUses(..., false)` states in
EndOfLine	11:90	\r\n
Comment	12:1	//     the C++ ring.
EndOfLine	12:21	\r\n
Comment	13:1	//
EndOfLine	13:3	\r\n
Comment	14:1	// The second shape is why the fixture exists: with the enclosing pass naming a lambda's
EndOfLine	14:89	\r\n
Comment	15:1	// declarations, the *second* lambda's `value` came out renamed (`_sm_value_2`) in one
EndOfLine	15:87	\r\n
Comment	16:1	// ring and untouched in the other. A case with the name reused across two lambdas is
EndOfLine	16:86	\r\n
Comment	17:1	// the only thing that shows it, and it is cheap enough to keep.
EndOfLine	17:65	\r\n
EndOfLine	18:1	\r\n
